1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,283
Smart Rearrangement. Same numbers, a smarter order. Let's make

2
00:00:04,283 --> 00:00:06,491
rational number addition easier.

3
00:00:06,850 --> 00:00:11,349
Long before calculators, people combined fractional quantities by hand

4
00:00:11,349 --> 00:00:15,145
— in trade, measurement, and record-keeping. Here is a

5
00:00:15,145 --> 00:00:18,801
simple ledger of measurements. As entries pile up, the

6
00:00:18,801 --> 00:00:22,386
calculation starts to look messy. Can we calculate this

7
00:00:22,386 --> 00:00:23,581
more cleverly?

8
00:00:23,933 --> 00:00:28,157
Here is our expression. Two fifths, plus three sevenths,

9
00:00:28,157 --> 00:00:32,230
minus six fifths, minus thirteen sevenths. The usual way

10
00:00:32,230 --> 00:00:35,322
is to work left to right, converting every pair

11
00:00:35,322 --> 00:00:38,792
to a common denominator of thirty five. Two fifths

12
00:00:38,792 --> 00:00:42,790
plus three sevenths becomes twenty nine over thirty five.

13
00:00:42,790 --> 00:00:45,580
Bring in minus six fifths, and we get minus

14
00:00:45,580 --> 00:00:50,332
thirteen over thirty five. Finally, minus thirteen sevenths gives

15
00:00:50,332 --> 00:00:53,877
minus seventy eight over thirty five. Correct — but

16
00:00:53,877 --> 00:00:56,064
that is a lot of repeated work.

17
00:00:56,417 --> 00:01:00,621
Let's look again, before calculating. Two of these fractions

18
00:01:00,621 --> 00:01:03,955
already share the same denominator — five. And two

19
00:01:03,955 --> 00:01:07,361
of them already share the same denominator — seven.

20
00:01:07,361 --> 00:01:10,551
If we group the friendly pairs together first, the

21
00:01:10,551 --> 00:01:12,508
work becomes much simpler.

22
00:01:12,867 --> 00:01:16,095
This works because addition lets us change the order

23
00:01:16,095 --> 00:01:18,442
of the terms. A plus B equals B plus

24
00:01:18,442 --> 00:01:21,523
A — the commutative property. Watch two of our

25
00:01:21,523 --> 00:01:24,678
terms swap places without changing the total.

26
00:01:25,033 --> 00:01:29,071
Addition also lets us change the grouping. Open bracket

27
00:01:29,071 --> 00:01:32,159
A plus B close bracket, plus C, equals A,

28
00:01:32,159 --> 00:01:34,851
plus open bracket B plus C close bracket —

29
00:01:34,851 --> 00:01:40,155
the associative property. Together, reordering and regrouping are the

30
00:01:40,155 --> 00:01:43,084
two moves behind smart rearrangement.

31
00:01:43,450 --> 00:01:46,611
Let's apply both moves to our example. Two fifths,

32
00:01:46,611 --> 00:01:50,760
three sevenths, minus six fifths, minus thirteen sevenths. Move

33
00:01:50,760 --> 00:01:53,395
minus six fifths next to two fifths, and minus

34
00:01:53,395 --> 00:01:56,819
thirteen sevenths next to three sevenths. Now group each

35
00:01:56,819 --> 00:01:58,861
friendly pair inside brackets.

36
00:01:59,217 --> 00:02:02,247
Two fifths minus six fifths gives minus four fifths,

37
00:02:02,247 --> 00:02:05,541
and three sevenths minus thirteen sevenths gives minus ten

38
00:02:05,541 --> 00:02:09,560
sevenths. There's still one common denominator step left: minus

39
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:12,722
twenty eight over thirty five, minus fifty over thirty

40
00:02:12,722 --> 00:02:15,950
five. That gives minus seventy eight over thirty five

41
00:02:15,950 --> 00:02:18,388
— the same answer, with far less work.

42
00:02:18,750 --> 00:02:22,037
Here's why this matters outside the textbook. A weather

43
00:02:22,037 --> 00:02:25,261
station records four temperature changes: plus one and a

44
00:02:25,261 --> 00:02:28,484
half, minus half, plus one and three quarters, minus

45
00:02:28,484 --> 00:02:31,578
three quarters degrees. To find the net change, we

46
00:02:31,578 --> 00:02:34,865
can group compatible fractions regardless of the order they

47
00:02:34,865 --> 00:02:37,444
happened in. One and a half minus half gives

48
00:02:37,444 --> 00:02:40,409
one, and one and three quarters minus three quarters

49
00:02:40,409 --> 00:02:43,374
gives one as well. Together, that's a net change

50
00:02:43,374 --> 00:02:44,921
of two degrees Celsius.

51
00:02:45,283 --> 00:02:49,287
Now it's your turn. Five eighths, minus one third,

52
00:02:49,287 --> 00:02:52,891
minus one eighth, plus two thirds. Look for the

53
00:02:52,891 --> 00:02:56,654
friendly pairs, and try grouping them yourself.

54
00:03:05,467 --> 00:03:08,886
First, pair the eighths: five eighths minus one eighth

55
00:03:08,886 --> 00:03:12,442
gives four eighths, which simplifies to one half. Then

56
00:03:12,442 --> 00:03:15,315
pair the thirds: minus one third plus two thirds

57
00:03:15,315 --> 00:03:18,529
gives one third. Add those together, and one half

58
00:03:18,529 --> 00:03:20,718
plus one third gives five sixths.

59
00:03:21,083 --> 00:03:24,432
Let's remember the two moves. A plus B equals

60
00:03:24,432 --> 00:03:27,536
B plus A — reorder. A, plus open bracket

61
00:03:27,536 --> 00:03:30,721
B plus C close bracket, equals open bracket A

62
00:03:30,721 --> 00:03:34,152
plus B close bracket, plus C — regroup. Same

63
00:03:34,152 --> 00:03:38,317
sum, smarter route. Thank you so much for watching,

64
00:03:38,317 --> 00:03:41,094
and I will see you in the next lesson!
